    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-18279",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix",
      "problem": "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+4|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-0)+2(0+1)+4(0+3) \\\\  & =6+2+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-4-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
