    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-104439",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity",
      "problem": "The entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & k & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=k-4.$ Find the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-4\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-4}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{22}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{k-4} \\\\ k-4 & =-4 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
