    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-319420",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix",
      "problem": "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n${a}_{31}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(2+1)-0\\,(0-1)+2\\,(-0-2) \\\\  & =3-0-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}^{t}={c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
