    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-319859",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity",
      "problem": "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\,(0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot k)+(-1)\\,(0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =(2+1)-2\\,(-k)+(-1)\\,(-k) \\\\  & =3+2k+k \\\\  & =3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-(4-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3k+3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3k+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3}{3k+3} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3k-3 & =-6 \\\\ -3k & =-6+3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
